 SAFETY PRECAUTIONS

         WARNING: LIKE ALL MACHINERY THERE IS POTENTIAL DANGER
         WHEN OPERATING THIS MACHINE. ACCIDENTS ARE FREQUENTLY
         CAUSED BY LACK OF FAMILIARITY OR FAILURE TO PAY ATTENTION.
         USE THIS MACHINE WITH RESPECT AND CAUTION TO DECREASE
         THE RISK OF OPERATOR INJURY. IF NORMAL SAFETY
         PRECAUTIONS ARE OVERLOOKED OR IGNORED, SERIOUS
         PERSONAL INJURY MAY OCCUR.

To protect against serious injury, use common sense and observe the following
precautions when using this product. CLARKE International is not responsible for
misuse of the equipment.

1. READ THE ENTIRE MANUAL BEFORE USE. Machinery presents serious injury
    hazards to untrained users.

2. WEAR PROPER APPAREL. DO NOT wear loose clothing, gloves, neckties,
    rings, or jewellery that can catch in moving parts. Wear protective hair covering to
    contain long hair and wear non-slip footwear.

3. NEVER OPERATE MACHINERY WHEN TIRED OR UNDER THE INFLUENCE
    OF DRUGS OR ALCOHOL. Be mentally alert at all times when in use.

4. ONLY ALLOW TRAINED AND PROPERLY SUPERVISED PERSONNEL TO
    OPERATE MACHINERY. Make sure operation instructions are safe and
    understood.

5. KEEP CHILDREN AND VISITORS AWAY from the work area.

6. KEEP THE WORK AREA CLEAN AND WELL LIT. Clutter and dark shadows
    may cause accidents.

7. MAINTAIN MACHINERY WITH CARE. Keep blades sharp and clean for best
    and safest performance. Check for misalignment or binding of moving parts,
    broken parts, or any condition that may affect the tools operation. If damaged,
    have the tool repaired before use.

8. CHECK FOR DAMAGED PARTS BEFORE USING MACHINERY. Check for
    binding or misaligned parts, broken parts, loose bolts, and any other conditions
    that may impair machine operation. Repair or replace damaged parts before
    operation.

9. OVERLOADING. Attempting to overload this machine beyond the capacities
    specified in the “Specifications” on page 2 could cause personal injury or property
    damage. DO NOT extend the hand crank to apply additional force.

10. USAGE. To avoid injury or property damage, always use the machine for its
    intended purposes, DO NOT modify the machine in any way.

                                                       3
11. METAL EDGES. Sharp metal edges can cut your fingers. ALWAYS chamfer and
    de-burr sharp sheet metal edges before bending and after cutting the workpiece.

12. ALWAYS hold the workpiece securely. Large work pieces should always be well
    supported to avoid loss of control.

13. PINCHING. This machine represents severe pinching and amputation hazards.
    ALWAYS keep hands away from the rollers, brake fingers, clamping bar and
    shearing blades when operating.

14. BODY POSITION. To avoid injury due to slipping, ALWAYS maintain secure
    footing and a comfortable body position when using this machine.

15. GOOD WORKING CONDITION. To reduce the risk of personal injury, ALWAYS
    inspect the working parts of this machine for cracks, burrs, loose fasteners or any
    other damage and resolve any issue before beginning operation.

16. WORKBENCH MOUNTING. Personal injury could occur if this machine should
    unexpectedly move during operation. ALWAYS make sure the machine is
    securely mounted to a stable workbench or stand that can support the weight and
    pressures of the operation.

17. ENTANGLEMENT HAZARDS. The moving parts of this machine represent
    entanglement hazards. DO NOT wear jewelry, or loose clothing, and tie back long
    hair when using this machine.

18. SHEARING BLADES/BRAKE FINGERS. If not properly aligned, the shearing
    blades or brake fingers can cause machine parts or the workpiece to break up and
    fly towards the operator. ALWAYS keep these parts properly adjusted and in
    good working condition.

         CAUTION: NO LIST OF SAFETY GUIDELINES CAN BE COMPLETE. EVERY
         WORKSHOP ENVIRONMENT IS DIFFERENT. ALWAYS CONSIDER SAFETY
         FIRST, AS IT APPLIES TO YOUR INDIVIDUAL WORKING CONDITIONS. USE
         THIS AND OTHER MACHINERY WITH CAUTION AND RESPECT. FAILURE TO
         DO SO COULD RESULT IN SERIOUS PERSONAL INJURY, DAMAGE TO
         EQUIPMENT, OR POOR WORK RESULTS

